\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Degenerates,\u8221\'94 Vigram spat. \u8220\'93Degenerates are everywhere. I tell you, they will not rest until they corrupt the sacred Word.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandrameen crinkled his nose. No arguments, please. Arguments with Vigram never ended. Two weeks could go by, and he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d pick it right back up. And he wasn\u8217\'92t some skinny person with a scholar\u8217\'92s neck, one of those people with a mind so deep in thought that they forget to eat. Vigram, well, Vigram had never missed a meal in his life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Burly, broad-faced Vigram. A bald head and belly beard. He pounded his rich breakfast table with his fat hand. When his slaves streamed out of the kitchen ferrying steaming plates of mush, he slid back into his rant about degenerate culture. The man was upset about the fact that words could mean different things to different people.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A Solitant priest hears the word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and dreams of the pure white glow of Solraheen. Hrkan folk hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of supplication, for the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship are as white as white can be. Outland pokes hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and they dream of daggering down those plain and stainless robes. And as it is with white, so it is with words, they may evoke discordant feelings. It is some wonder then, that Solraheen would choose to use such an equivocal thing as a word to create the world. He dwelt in the void, spoke the sacred Word, the world emerged, and people haven\u8217\'92t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 One might be led to believe that Solraheen chose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant. But this is false, or so Vigram claimed. Words are fixed, or at least, they were.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The word \u8216\'91art\u8217\'92,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, \u8220\'93is the best example.\u8221\'94 His voice was deep and provincial, though he tried very hard to adopt a refined air. He said, \u8220\'93When Solraheen walked the earth an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Besides, he did not want to argue, or philosophize, he wanted to eat. And Vigram didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t have the face for this sort of conversation. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandrameen crinkled his nose. He did not want to argue, or philosophize, he wanted to eat. Besides, Vigram didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t have the face for this sort of conversation. No broad-faced man with a gleaming scalp and belly beard could ever have something worthwhile to say. This was an extremely shallow position to hold, but Mandri held it because he could not escape the sense that worthwhile thoughts could only come from skinny people with scholar\u8217\'92s necks\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 people with minds so active that they forget to eat. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram, meanwhile, smacked his rich breakfast table with a fat hand. Then he slid into a rant about degenerate culture while his slaves ferried steaming plates of mush from out of his kitchen.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Degenerates,\u8221\'94 Vigram argued, \u8220\'93corrupt the sacred Word.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He was upset about the fact that words could mean different things to different people.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A Solitant priest hears the word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and dreams of the pure white glow of Solraheen. Hrkan folk hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of supplication, for the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship are as white as white can be. Outland pokes hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and they dream of daggering down those plain and stainless robes. And as it is with white, so it is with words, they may evoke discordant feelings. It is some wonder then, that Solraheen would choose to use such an equivocal thing as a word to create the world. He dwelt in the void, spoke the sacred Word, the world emerged, and people haven\u8217\'92t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 One might be led to believe that Solraheen chose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant. But this is false, or so Vigram claimed. Words are fixed, or at least, they were.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The word \u8216\'91art\u8217\'92,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, \u8220\'93is the best example.\u8221\'94 His voice was deep and provincial, though he tried very hard to adopt a refined air. He said, \u8220\'93When Solraheen walked the earth an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The plates were all on the table now, but Mandri was forced to wait. Vigram, the host, was meant to eat first. If only he would stop talking. Mandri lost hope when Vigram began to list the great artworks of \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91undiluted culture\u8217\'92. Eventually, the words \u8216\'91Art as exegetical instrument\u8217\'92 issued from Vigram\u8217\'92s mouth, and Mandri had stop himself. After, Vigram tried to extract some concession through brute interrogation:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What is art?\u8221\'94 Vigram asked. \u8220\'93Who is an artist? Who isn\u8217\'92t?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram leaned back in his creaky chair like he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d just gotten to bottom of every important question. Mandri was careful to avoid engaging, because the food was }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 right there}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 . }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Who }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 is}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  an artist?\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 Vigram asked again. \u8220\'93A painter, a sculptor, these are artists, yes, that\u8217\'92s reasonable to say, but what about a tax collector? Is he an artist too if he plumbs the depths of greed? Is a sheep-shearer plying her shears an artist if she\u8217\'92s sufficiently clever about it? What about a farmer? If you\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 let me ask you this, Mandri\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 if you were to give a young man a shovel, a field of dirt, friends to impress, what would he do with it?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Any young man?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes. What would he do with a shovel and field of dirt? He can do anything, but what does he do?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The average young man?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well, I suppose he\u8217\'92d dig a penis.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93He would, wouldn\u8217\'92t he!\u8221\'94 Vigram said, laughing. \u8220\'93Yes, I dare say he would dig is a penis. The average Jandri boy will find it funny to dig a big old ugly in the dirt. He laughs because it doubles as a drainage ditch, just like the real thing! And I would say that this is the crux of the matter\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 to place such degeneracy in the same category as the cloud of colored windows which open up the ceiling of the Gambagall\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I say! I say, }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 that}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  is an affront to the sacred. And dare I say it, the Solitants are right! If everyone is an artist, then the word is as useful as an unmarked weight!\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram clutched his bearded jaw and winced. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d taken his first bite of some incomprehensible appetizer drenched in gravy, but the dish did not accommodate his troublesome tooth. None of the food here did. This was his leech farm, and so every dish\u8217\'92s principal ingredient was some variation of the Meskalimi leech. This particular leech was a prized delicacy throughout the Hrkan world, as its meat, if it can be called meat, was quite sweet. It was also said to cure infertility, and somehow, fertility as well. It was also chewy. Each bite forced Vigram\u8217\'92s tongue into acrobatics. Two bites in, his tongue slipped, and the pain had him talking out the side of his cheek in an odd drawl. It hurt to hear the man speak, in more ways than one.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why not choose a different word?\u8221\'94 Mandri suggested as he dug into a bowl of leechy porridge.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Because words must have walls,\u8221\'94 Vigram said as he pushed his meal away. \u8220\'93The true artist, I say this, the true artist is a divine instrument. They must be kept apart from the self-skinny horde.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri chowed down on the cold, crumbled chunks of what he supposed was boiled leech. It was quite bitter on account of the overpowering sauce, but the effort to suppress a gag helped keep him from speaking his mind. It helped that he hadn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t eaten in two days.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You know,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, getting excited, \u8220\'93the Old Jandri word for \u8216\'91artist\u8217\'92 is lost to time, but there is a word in what\u8217\'92s left of that language fit for the task\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8216\'91bursheg\u8217\'92. It means something like \u8216\'91fertile ground\u8217\'92, but in the Solitant tradition it means \u8216\'91true artist\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Interesting,\u8221\'94 Mandri lied.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandrameen crinkled his nose. He did not want to argue, or philosophize. For one thing, Vigram didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t have the face for this sort of conversation\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it was too broad and burly. Altogether, Vigram was little more than a gleaming scalp, a belly beard, and a general sense that he was much too big for human skin. It was hard to move past all that. There was also a sort of perfunctory logic which said that important thoughts could only come from people with minds so active that they forget to eat. Vigram, meanwhile, smacked his rich breakfast table with a fat hand.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93If words can change,\u8221\'94 Vigram opined, \u8220\'93then words are as useful unmarked weights.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But words }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 can}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  change,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 Mandri said emphatically.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Nonsense,\u8221\'94 Vigram countered. \u8220\'93Words mean what they mean and it is degenerate to try and change them.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 Mandri said as he stalled for the sake of his composure. \u8220\'93This is simple. Think about it\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 a Solitant priest hears the word \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and thinks of the pure white glow of Solraheen; Hrkan folk hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and they think of the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship; Outland pokes hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of sparkling salt pans, or sun bleached bone. So, you see\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93They all think of the color,\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93Nothing more.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 It was rude to finish a sentence like that, but Mandri refrained from shaking his head because cornering Vigram required tact.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Alright, let\u8217\'92s try this: Solraheen created this world with a single word, yes?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Assuredly.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93He dwelt in the void, spoke one word, the world emerged, and guess what? People haven\u8217\'92t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t disagree.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well, you have to believe that this is intentional. Solraheen knew how words can acquire new meaning, or shed meaning, or maybe lose their meaning altogether. He never would have chosen to create with such an equivocal thing as a word if He didn\u8217\'92t want to play with what He meant. \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram grunted. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You have it all wrong,\u8221\'94 he said confidently. \u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t misunderstand me, boy\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I don\u8217\'92t mean to say that some words are not malleable }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 today}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 , in }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 our}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  time, because we live in a degenerate culture. I\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92m saying that is not how words }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 were}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 , or }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 should be}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 .\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t think it matters what you want, or what should be, there\u8217\'92s only what is.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But, do you not agree that a word without walls is as useful as an unmarked weight?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Again,\u8221\'94 Mandri said. \u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s not\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Take the word \u8216\'91art\u8217\'92 for example.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri groaned. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d been expecting this. Vigram always dovetailed the conversation back into the same damn thing they\u8217\'92d been arguing about for weeks. Art. Who gives a damn about art? Mandri sank his head into his hands.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Follow me,\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93Back when Solraheen walked the earth an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You sound like a Solitant.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I did go into the temple last week. Had to bother the whores, you know.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93And you listened to a sermon?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I should say that I did. Surprised?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well, you don\u8217\'92t normally go into the temple, because that means you\u8217\'92d have to listen to someone else speak.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But I did go.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93And now you\u8217\'92re inflicting this on me? That\u8217\'92s not fair. If I\u8217\'92d wanted to hear it, I would have gone myself.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93\u8216\'91Art as Exegetical Instrument\u8217\'92 was the sermon\u8217\'92s title.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram, please.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It was an invigorating sermon.The priestess, her style was to extract the truth of the matter through interrogation: She stood there on the dais, and said, \u8220\'93What is art? Who is an artist? Who isn\u8217\'92t?\u8221\'94 Like she was torturing us for the truth.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Because who }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 is}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  an artist? A painter, a sculptor, these are artists, yes, that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s reasonable to say, but what about a tax collector? Is he an artist too if he plumbs the depths of greed? Is a sheep-shearer plying her shears an artist if she\u8217\'92s sufficiently clever about it? What about a farmer? She said this: Give a young man a shovel, a field of dirt, friends to impress, and what does he do?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93He digs a penis,\u8221\'94 Mandri joked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The priestess left the derivation of the answer as an exercise to the audience for the sake of the prude, but yes, I would say that the answer is a penis. The average Jandri boy will find it funny to dig a big old ugly in the dirt. He laughs because it doubles as a drainage ditch; an imitation of the real thing. And I say, this is the crux of the matter\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 to place such degeneracy in the same category as the cloud of colored windows which open up the ceiling of the Gambagall\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I say! I say, }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 that}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  is an affront to the sacred.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram clutched his bearded jaw and winced. The food on his table did not accommodate his troublesome tooth. This was his leech farm, and so every dish\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s principal ingredient was some variation of the Meskalimi leech. This particular leech was a prized delicacy throughout the Hrkan world as its essence was said to cure infertility, and somehow, fertility as well. The meat, if it can be called meat, was quite sweet. It was also chewy. Every bite forced Vigram\u8217\'92s tongue into acrobatics. Two bites in, it slipped, and the pain had him talking out the side of his cheek in an odd drawl. It hurt to hear the man speak, in more ways than one.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well,\u8221\'94 Mandri suggested, \u8220\'93If you don\u8217\'92t want words to change, why not choose a different word?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Because words must have walls,\u8221\'94 Vigram said as he pushed his plate away. \u8220\'93The true artist, I say this, the true artist is a divine instrument. They must be kept apart from the self-skinny horde.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri chowed down on the cold, crumbled chunks of what he supposed was boiled leech. It was quite bitter on account of the overpowering sauce, but the effort to suppress a gag helped keep him from speaking his mind.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You know,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, getting excited, \u8220\'93the Old Jandri word for \u8216\'91artist\u8217\'92 is lost to time, but there is a word in what\u8217\'92s left of that language fit for the task\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8216\'91bursheg\u8217\'92. It means something like \u8216\'91fertile ground\u8217\'92, but in the Solitant tradition it means \u8216\'91true artist\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Interesting,\u8221\'94 Mandri lied.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ll tell you what\u8217\'92s interesting,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, lifting his thick brows. \u8220\'93The old witch who gave that sermon, she said that art has nothing to do with what a person makes. Have you ever heard that? I didn\u8217\'92t understand it at first, but you know, it\u8217\'92s true. True artistry, consider this, true artistry is a state of mind. Some of the greatest artists never made anything at all.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri stuffed his mouth with a breadroll in a futile attempt to stifle himself. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I was under the impression,\u8221\'94 he said as he worked the roll into his cheek, \u8220\'93that an artist makes art.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram frowned and tutted. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Nobody makes anything,\u8221\'94 he insisted. \u8220\'93We can only ever rearrange. When you paint, you push pigment around. How is that any different than my footprints rearranging sand? No, either everyone is an artist, or, and this is my claim, an artist is a certain kind}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 of}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 person. A certain kind of }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 mind}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 . You know, too many big ideas, too many shallow excuses, that sort of thing. It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a mind marked by excess\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 not abundance\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 excess}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 .\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t look at me,\u8221\'94 Mandri said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram smiled. The fat swerve of his bottom lip was a baited hook. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93So exaggerated is the artist\u8217\'92s sense of self that it becomes impossible to describe them without resorting to sarcasm.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re still looking at me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s smile swerved. \u8220\'93There is no one else here.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri glanced at the two slaves standing at attention by the kitchen\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s double-swing doors. Vigram\u8217\'92s blindness to their existence was cruel, but this did not dismantle his point, as Mandri did think highly of himself.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Why shouldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t he?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 When he was ten, he could paint a portrait in oils that rivaled the old Jeketian masters. These weren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t polish-jobs where the paint was thin and every hair was rendered into being like he\u8217\'92d lost the plot of reality. By his hand, a single stroke of umber was enough to define the chestnut skin, the high-arched cheek, the skull underneath, the light, the shadow, even the mood of the piece. Warms and cools in one single stroke! With twenty-four, he could find a vivid likeness. There might be a few extra nibbles to make a beauty\u8217\'92s eyes glisten and her cleavage heave, but nobody counted those. Well, Vigram counted them. Lascivious men counted them. Probably, every man counted them. Some women, presumably.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And like any prideful artist, Mandri would strut around the grand Hrkan galleries and make clear his contempt for his contemporaries. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d stand in front of a painting and say, \u8220\'93This piece really }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 moves}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  me,\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 and he\u8217\'92d make a show of walking away. All must bear witness to his mop of black hair as it flounced itself in time with his skinny, stiff-backed step. Or he\u8217\'92d say, \u8220\'93I weep at every stroke,\u8221\'94 and he\u8217\'92d flash a knowing smile. Back in those days, he\u8217\'92d say these things directly to a fellow artist\u8217\'92s face if he could get to it. He never thought of it as bullying because artists, like himself, were safe targets for playful derision; it wasn\u8217\'92t the person he was making fun of, it was their failed attempts to express themselves, and those were }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 completely}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  different things. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d never go after the person.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 No, never.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Artists were the best people. They needed tough skin, unearned confidence, and a flair for the dramatic. Without any of that, they expire in obscurity. Then there is the servility. Above all, an artist must be servile to the hand that feeds. And look at that, Vigram had a lot of food at his table.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram,\u8221\'94 Mandri said confidently, \u8220\'93you are an idiot.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram choked on a wad of smushed leech. He coughed, pounded his chest, then coughed again. Once recovered, he glared at Mandri with furious, protuberant eyes, oblivious to the brown specks of food in his long beard. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93In whose house do we sit?!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yours.\u8221\'94 Mandri said. He mustered a disarming smile. \u8220\'93But when it comes to these things, when it comes to matters of art, I am correct. If I am not correct, then you certainly aren\u8217\'92t.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why? Because I like some painters who aren\u8217\'92t you?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t paint anymore, so I don\u8217\'92t care who you like.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93If you\u8217\'92re not a painter, then why do I pay you for lessons?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93These lessons are a charity.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mandri, I }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 pay}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  you!\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Even so.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Leech juice dripped down the corner of Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s lip, accenting his frown. He wiped it away. \u8220\'93Hah!\u8221\'94 he exclaimed as he broke into a jolly smile. He stabbed his clam shell into a bowl of fermented leech. So much lard was mushed into those leeches that the gray paste could grease a wheel. Vigram\u8217\'92s outstretched tongue batted at the stringy slop before he slumped it all down his gullet. He patted his fat lips dry with his fifth napkin, then threw that napkin onto the floor to join the others.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We both know,\u8221\'94 he said, \u8220\'93that you only come here for the money.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Then we are both mistaken.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram rolled his eyes and set his leech lard mush aside. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Listen,\u8221\'94 he said. He put on his business face, which looked something like a spooked coconut. \u8220\'93You have a gift. I should say that I believe in your talent. You should believe me when I say that success is there for the taking. I know you don\u8217\'92t want to debase yourself by doing what it takes to succeed, but the money is easy. It is }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 sitting}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  there for the taking. People }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 want}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  to give it to you. A young man such as you, I cannot understand why you won\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t even try.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri settled into his chair because he knew what was coming next: Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s life story. It always came out as a sort of instructional, as if Mandri could do it, too.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The first step in the accumulation of wealth was often left out because not everyone could easily murder their father to inherit a sizable sum. That being said, four ounces of silver would do.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Next, loan those four ounces out to a leech farm. Why a leech farm? Beyond the blunt symbolism of the leech, there\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s not much of a reason. It is important to remember that not every leech farm is the same. Look for a farm that supplies a Solitant temple with leeches for their bloodletting rituals. That\u8217\'92s a surefire bet because temples are always the last thing the mob will torch in lean times. When the lean times hit especially hard, when the only bread available is made from fern-roots, the leech farm will feel the squeeze. They\u8217\'92ll ask for leniency on the interest rate. Do not budge. Take over the farm. The leechmen might accuse you of committing usury against Solraheen Himself, because in some small way you are, but Solraheen is dead and so what does it matter? Get together with a priest and pray about it around a jug of beer, flash him a slip of silver, and the bleary shine will blind him to all the world\u8217\'92s iniquity.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 With the farm in hand, begin to lend smaller amounts over shorter terms to farmers who need emergency loans to pay their taxes after droughts or infestations. Split these debts up and sell them off in Meskalim\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s bond market. There\u8217\'92s not much money in Meskalim itself, but it has a busy port and that means access to all the important markets of the Hrkan world.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 From there, it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a simple matter of reducing your eyes to tiny pinpricks, ridding yourself of limbs, segmenting the body, and now that you\u8217\'92ve transformed into a leech, you may attach firmly to the continent\u8217\'92s rump and drink your fill. Anyone who tries to shake you off before you\u8217\'92ve finished becomes your slave. You pick out the salvageable fools for your own use, and now you\u8217\'92ve got a brood of leeches pouring over lists of sums credited and debts owed. So long as you maintain a layer of abstraction between these creatures and your henchmen, it\u8217\'92s all self-sustaining.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Now you have a comfortable life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Idle hours.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No debasement necessary,\u8221\'94 Vigram added before licking his}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  plump fingers clean of sun-dried leech oil. This oil was a true delicacy, squeezed as it was from the salivary glands of leeches aged twenty years or more. Leeches that big are more devourers than bloodsuckers, so they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re much more expensive to feed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Now,\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 You know all about the making of a painting. That\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s why I respect you. I know all about the making of money. At a certain point you have to join them up, because the world doesn\u8217\'92t work how it should. The world works how it works. You }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 have}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  to make a living. Success, Mandri, is a very sweet thing.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He clapped his meaty hands, prompting one of the nameless slaves to come and take his plates. Once done, he stood up. His chair protested the sudden shift of his massive weight with a terrible squeak.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We should begin,\u8221\'94 he said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri nodded, then wiped his own mouth and excused himself from the table. He took his time, because he wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t looking forward to beginning anything. But, he had to. He needed the money.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0}